# Cold email standard

REPLACE THIS FILE with your own standard - the document you already
generated. Whatever is in here is exactly what every email gets marked
against, and nothing else. If a rule is not written here, no producer
will ever be marked down for breaking it.

The clearer and more specific each line is, the more useful the grades.
"Be personalised" is hard to grade consistently. "The first sentence
must refer to something specific about the recipient's company, not
their industry in general" grades the same way every time.

Below is a placeholder so the grader runs. Delete it.

## 1. Subject line
- Six to eight words, sentence case.
- Says what the email is about. No curiosity-gap teasers.

## 2. Opening
- First sentence refers to something specific and verifiable about the
  recipient's company.
- Never opens with "I hope this email finds you well" or a description
  of our own company.

## 3. The offer
- One clear reason this is worth the recipient's time, stated in terms
  of their situation rather than our product.
- At most one proof point, and it must be concrete.

## 4. The ask
- Exactly one, at the end, answerable yes or no.
- Asks for a short conversation, never for a purchase.

## 5. Length and tone
- Under 150 words.
- Plain English. No jargon, no superlatives, no exclamation marks.
- Written as one person to another, not as a company to a market.

## 6. Signature
- Real name, real company, real phone number.

## 7. Follow-ups
The grader is told which touch it is reading, so write this section for the
follow-ups specifically. It only applies to chasing someone who has not
replied - once they answer, the email is a conversation and is not graded.

- Shorter than the first email. Under 60 words.
- Never re-introduces the company from scratch. They already have that email.
- Gives a NEW reason to reply. "Just bumping this up" is not a reason.
- Never guilt-trips ("I notice you haven't responded").
- Same single ask, or an easier one.
